NAME
       shutdown - stop, power off or reboot the machine, now or later

SYNOPSIS
       shutdown [-r | -h | -P | -H] [TIME] [MESSAGE]
       shutdown -c

DESCRIPTION
       shutdown brings the system down cleanly: services are stopped in
       order, file systems are unmounted, then the machine powers off (the
       default) or restarts (-r). On RHEL 9 it asks systemd to do the work.

       TIME is "now", +M (in M minutes) or HH:MM (that time of day, 24-hour
       clock). Without TIME, shutdown waits one minute. While a shutdown is
       scheduled, logged-in users get a warning, and new logins are refused
       in the last five minutes. MESSAGE is added to the warning.

       Only root can run shutdown (or a user allowed by sudo).

OPTIONS
       -r, --reboot
              Restart the machine.

       -h     Power off (the same as -P, the default).

       -P, --poweroff
              Power off.

       -H, --halt
              Stop the system but leave the power on.

       -c     Cancel a scheduled shutdown.

EXAMPLES
       Reboot now:

           shutdown -r now

       Power off in ten minutes, with a warning to the users:

           shutdown +10 "Disk replacement, back at 14:00"

       Reboot at 23:30, then change your mind:

           shutdown -r 23:30
           shutdown -c

       The same as systemctl commands, without a delay:

           systemctl reboot
           systemctl poweroff

SEE ALSO
       reboot(8), poweroff(8), halt(8), systemctl(1), wall(1)
